      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, I'm glad the explanation was helpful. When looking at a diagram, a good tip to remember which part is which is to think of Bernoulli's principle as the \"speedy stream\" and Newton's third law as the \"deflective force.\" Bernoulli's principle is all about the speed of the air over the wing creating a pressure difference, so look for the areas where the air is moving faster or slower. This will be indicated by the curvature of the air flow lines; they will be more curved or straight depending on the speed of the air.\n\nNewton's third law is about the deflection of the air, so look for the direction of the air flow as it leaves the wing. The air is deflected downwards, so you'll see the air flow lines pointing downwards away from the wing. This deflection creates an upward force, which is the lift.\n\nRemembering these two concepts as \"speedy stream\" and \"deflective force\" can help you quickly identify which principle is at play in a given part of the diagram. Good luck with your exam, and may the winds of knowledge be ever in your favor!",
